//? Title: Estimate Gaussian model evidence by two routes
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Compare proposals using bridge sampling

A parameter has a standard normal prior. We observe 2 with Gaussian
measurement noise of variance 1. The posterior mean is 1 and its variance
is 0.5. Integrating the parameter gives a normal observation density with
mean 0 and variance 2, so the exact log evidence is available.

Bridge sampling evaluates the joint and the normalized proposal on both
posterior and proposal draws. The two proposals below have the same
variance but different centers. The overlap estimate and effective weight
counts help show how many draws contribute to the bridge.

```{cpp, id=normal-bridge, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/bayes/evidence.hpp>
#include <datamunge/random/random.hpp>
#include <cmath>
#include <numbers>
#include <print>

int main() {
    using namespace datamunge;
    constexpr std::size_t count = 24000;
    random::SplitMix64 rng(372);
    const auto log_normal = [](double x, double mean, double variance) {
        const double residual = x - mean;
        return -0.5 * (std::log(2 * std::numbers::pi * variance) + residual * residual / variance);
    };
    std::vector<double> posterior_draws;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < count; ++i)
        posterior_draws.push_back(1 + rng.normal() / std::numbers::sqrt2);
    const auto evaluate = [&](double x, double center) {
        return bayes::BridgeLogDensities{log_normal(x, 0, 1) + log_normal(2, x, 1),
                                       log_normal(x, center, 1.21)};
    };
    const auto show = [&](const char* label, double center) {
        std::vector<bayes::BridgeLogDensities> posterior, proposal;
        for (double draw : posterior_draws) {
            posterior.push_back(evaluate(draw, center));
            proposal.push_back(evaluate(center + 1.1 * rng.normal(), center));
        }
        const auto fit = bayes::bridge_sampling(posterior, proposal);
        std::println("| {} | {:.4f} | {:.3f} | {:.0f} | {:.0f} | {} |", label, fit.log_evidence,
                     fit.overlap, fit.posterior_weight_ess, fit.proposal_weight_ess, fit.converged);
    };
    std::println("Exact log evidence {:.4f}.\n", log_normal(2, 0, 2));
    std::println("| Proposal | Log evidence | Overlap | Posterior weight ESS | Proposal weight ESS | Converged |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |");
    show("Center 0.7", 0.7);
    show("Center 4", 4);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Exact log evidence -2.2655.

| Proposal | Log evidence | Overlap | Posterior weight ESS | Proposal weight ESS | Converged |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |
| Center 0.7 | -2.2646 | 0.904 | 22894 | 20652 | true |
| Center 4 | -2.2896 | 0.141 | 4428 | 2752 | true |
// result_end

The shifted proposal places less mass near the posterior. Its fixed-point
iteration can converge even with much smaller effective weight counts.

[Meng and Wong (1996), equation 4.1|https://www3.stat.sinica.edu.tw/statistica/j6n4/j6n43/j6n43.htm]

> Integrate along the power-posterior ladder

The power posterior at inverse temperature t raises the likelihood to t.
At t = 0 it is the prior, and at t = 1 it is the posterior. Integrating
the expected original log likelihood across these temperatures yields the
log evidence. Its derivative with respect to t is the variance of the
original log likelihood.

This Gaussian model gives the two moments directly at each temperature.
Using exact moments lets the example isolate quadrature error. For
sampled power posteriors, the library can compute the moments from the
log-likelihood draws.

```{cpp, id=normal-power-ladder, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/bayes/evidence.hpp>
#include <cmath>
#include <numbers>
#include <print>

int main() {
    using namespace datamunge::bayes;
    const double log_normal_constant = std::log(2 * std::numbers::pi);
    const double exact = -0.5 * (std::log(4 * std::numbers::pi) + 2);
    std::println("Exact log evidence {:.7f}.\n", exact);
    std::println("| Intervals | Trapezoidal | Corrected | Correction magnitude | Corrected absolute error |");
    std::println("| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    for (int intervals : {2, 4, 8, 16}) {
        std::vector<PowerPosteriorRung> ladder;
        for (int i = 0; i <= intervals; ++i) {
            const double temperature = static_cast<double>(i) / intervals;
            const double variance = 1 / (1 + temperature);
            const double residual_squared = 4 * variance * variance;
            ladder.push_back({temperature, -0.5 * (log_normal_constant + variance + residual_squared),
                              0.5 * variance * variance + residual_squared * variance});
        }
        const auto fit = thermodynamic_integration(ladder);
        std::println("| {} | {:.7f} | {:.7f} | {:.7f} | {:.7f} |", intervals, fit.trapezoidal,
                     fit.log_evidence, fit.discretization_error, std::abs(fit.log_evidence - exact));
    }
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Exact log evidence -2.2655121.

| Intervals | Trapezoidal | Corrected | Correction magnitude | Corrected absolute error |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 2 | -2.3425496 | -2.2618205 | 0.0807292 | 0.0036916 |
| 4 | -2.2854380 | -2.2652557 | 0.0201823 | 0.0002564 |
| 8 | -2.2705412 | -2.2654956 | 0.0050456 | 0.0000165 |
| 16 | -2.2667725 | -2.2655111 | 0.0012614 | 0.0000010 |
// result_end

The correction estimates the ordinary trapezoidal discretization error.
The last column measures the error remaining in the corrected estimate
for this model.

[Friel, Hurn and Wyse (2014), section 2.1|https://arxiv.org/abs/1209.3198]
